 "docs": {
  "en": {
   "title": "Les Deux Alpes: 45 cm at the top, zero snow at the base",
   "description": "On 4 November, Les Deux Alpes has 45 centimetres at the summit but zero at the base—an extreme early-season height gap.",
   "lede": "Les Deux Alpes today shows the extreme height divide of early November: 45 centimetres on the upper slopes but zero snow at the base. This gap determines where you can ski right now.",
   "sections": [
    {
     "h": "The height divide",
     "p": [
      "Starting mountain work in the autumn, the freezing level tells the story first. Today at Les Deux Alpes, it sits between 2,310 and 2,350 metres. Above that line, snow falls and sticks; below it, rain washes the ground. So we measure 45 centimetres on the upper slopes at 3,511 metres, but nothing on the base at 1,284 metres—a vertical gap of over two kilometres.",
      "This is not unusual for early November in the high Alps. The dry mountains of southern France follow this pattern: summits white, valleys brown. But it does shape what is skiable. Terrain below 2,400 metres is thin or bare. Upper slopes hold the snow. If you are planning a trip, height matters completely right now."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "Thinner than usual",
     "p": [
      "The real issue emerges at mid-height. At around 2,397 metres—a typical mid-slope altitude—our snow model shows zero centimetres today. In a normal year, looking back at 46 past winters, this date sees about 8 centimetres. So Les Deux Alpes is running below average for November. We are deeper than only 8 of those past 46 years, putting this season in the bottom tier for early snow.",
      "This kind of early-season thinness happens in some years. Warm air and rain dominate the first weeks of November. The mountains stay largely brown until December breaks the pattern. The next three days will tell us if this year follows that path or if the forecast snow changes the picture."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "The forecast window",
     "p": [
      "Relief is coming. Our snow model predicts 24 centimetres of new snow across 5–7 November. November 6 brings the heaviest snow, with 18 centimetres. But the air stays warm through 6 November: the freezing level reaches 2,640 metres on 5 November and 2,360 metres on 6 November. So much of the new snow deposits on the summits while the mid-slopes gain snow more slowly.",
      "By 7 November the air chills and the freezing level drops to around 2,260 metres maximum. Snow will reach mid-height more reliably. But the base of the resort will likely stay dry. Rocks and thin cover remain hazards on lower runs for days after new snow arrives."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "Snow at each height",
     "p": [
      "At the summit (3,511 m), 45 centimetres today plus another 29 centimetres forecast means good skiing for those who can reach upper terrain. The mid-slopes around 2,397 metres have zero now but will gain about 16 centimetres by 7 November as new snow pushes down. That is still thin; rocks are a real risk. The base at 1,284 metres stays bare on the model, with zero centimetres forecast. Rocks dominate the base terrain.",
      "If you plan to visit Les Deux Alpes in the next three days, focus on the upper slopes and ridges. If you have flexibility, waiting until 7 November—when the air chills and snow reaches mid-height more reliably—gives you better skiing."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "The wider Alps",
     "p": [
      "Les Deux Alpes sits in a wider pattern across the Alps. Right now, 760 resorts have snow cover out of 1,178, based on the satellite view from 1 November. The region shows 43,150 square kilometres under snow. La Grave, across the valley, has 42 centimetres on the upper slopes. Orelle to the south has 41 centimetres. The story repeats everywhere: early season, high summits only, bare bases. The forecast draws the same pattern across the range; most new snow will land above 2,400 metres."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "What this means now",
     "p": [
      "If you visit Les Deux Alpes in the next few days, upper slopes are your terrain. Check the official avalanche bulletin before any off-piste skiing, as thin early-season cover and new snow on old ground can hide hazards. The base is not ready yet. This is November skiing in the mountains: patience and elevation win."
     ]
    }
   ],
   "advice": [
    "Upper slopes at 3,511 metres hold 45 centimetres right now; this is your best terrain for the next three days.",
    "Avoid terrain below 2,400 metres until after 7 November. The base at 1,284 metres stays bare; rocks dominate there.",
    "November 6 brings 18 centimetres of new snow, but mainly on upper slopes above 2,400 metres. Watch for rocks as new snow covers old ground.",
    "Check the official avalanche bulletin before any off-piste skiing; thin early-season snow can hide hazards.",
    "If you have flexibility, waiting until 7 November gives better skiing throughout the resort, as the freezing level drops to around 2,260 metres."
   ],
   "captions": {
    "map": "Snow depth across the Alps on 4 November: 760 resorts have snow cover out of 1,178, with Les Deux Alpes, La Grave and Orelle all showing 41–45 centimetres on upper slopes and zero to minimal snow at lower elevations.",
    "season_chart": "This winter at Les Deux Alpes (line) against the usual range of past winters (band) and their middle (dashed).",
    "photo": "Les Deux Alpes on 4 November: 45 centimetres on the summit at 3,511 metres but bare ground at the base at 1,284 metres, showing the sharp height gradient of early season."
   }
